# Vauxhall Ten four-door saloon, 1937-1940 and 1946-1947.
#
# The Ten: launched in October 1937 with the first customer car on
# 1 November 1937, and the first British car of unitary construction.
# Production stopped in May 1940 when Luton turned to trucks and tanks, and
# the model returned in 1946 as the HIY before withdrawal in 1947, so wartime
# production is not included. The four-door saloon is modelled; the 1938-39
# separate-chassis coupe is not represented.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vauxhall_Ten
#
# Dimensions: length 155.5 in (3.950 m), width 61 in (1.549 m), wheelbase
# 94 in (2.388 m). The post-war car is 159 in long on a 97.75 in wheelbase.
# Height was not established and is estimated from the photograph.
#
# Shape reference: NO true level profile exists. Image 1 is about 40 degrees
# off the side and only 960 px wide, so the flank is a reasoned estimate
# within the published dimensions and the limitation is recorded.
#
# Colours: grade (d); no Vauxhall colour list was found. Black, Maroon, Cream
# and Dark Blue are observed colours. Weights 12, 10, 9, 8, total 39,
# provisional.
#
# Speed 100 km/h. Comfort 25/368, on the line 330 + 1.5 x 25. Classes 1,2.
# Dates: intro November 1937; retires staggered 1955-1958 as a road tail.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/vauxhall-ten.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/vauxhall-ten.dae. References:
# citycars/references/vauxhall-ten/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: the flank and the 1.600 m height are estimates from a
# 40-degree view. The master represents the pre-war H-type with its vertical
# grille and 2.388 m wheelbase; the 1946-47 HIY had a longer wheelbase and a
# horizontal grille and is not separately modelled.


